  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nA dominating ridge is once again providing very warm temperatures\nacross the area with heat indices reaching over 100 degrees this\nafternoon. \n\nA trough over southeast Canada will begin to suppress the ridge to\nthe southwest tomorrow but only slightly. Therefore, the heat\nadvisory remains in effect for the majority of the area through\nFriday. It is possible that the heat advisory will need expanded to\nthe north, but that will depend on overnight thunderstorm coverage\nand cloud cover. \n\nThere is the chance that southwest locations (southeast Indiana,\nsouthwest Ohio, and northern Kentucky), may need a heat advisory\nextension into Saturday. However, confidence is a bit higher that\nthunderstorm activity will move through Friday night into Saturday\nmorning, reducing the potential for ideal conditions that would\nsupport another day of heat. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nLatest satellite and radar imagery shows a few taller cumulus \nleading to isolated showers and thunderstorms this afternoon. The\nsevere threat would be low with this activity, with perhaps a very\nlocalized downburst or two. \n\nLate this afternoon and into the early evening, an MCS is likely to \nbe moving quickly across portions of northern/northeast Ohio.\nDamaging wind potential is higher in those locations, with SPC\nupgrading to an Enhanced (level 3 of 5) for the Day 1 Severe Weather\nOutlook. On the southern edge, an outflow boundary may stretch into \ncentral Ohio, providing the opportunity for more coverage within the \nlocal area. Severe weather parameters are strongest in the \nnortheast, with bulk shear values around 25-35 knots this evening. \nDepending on the ultimate coverage of thunderstorms along the \noutflow, the potential still exists for a few damaging wind gusts in \ncentral Ohio this evening. A severe thunderstorm watch has been \nissued for portions of central-Ohio to cover this potential. \nOtherwise, a few scattered thunderstorms may occur elsewhere along \nthe outflow as it moves through the area. \n\nThe next chance for thunderstorms across the local area is Friday,\nwith another shortwave working through the Great Lakes Friday\nmorning. A corridor of instability will exist over the local area\nuntil a thunderstorm complex moves through. So, depending on the\nactivity Friday, another round would be possible Friday night into\nSaturday morning with a stronger shortwave. As noted in previous\ndiscussions, multiple thunderstorm complexes are possible across the\nlocal area up until Saturday evening. Drier air will eventually work\ninto the region from the north as the ridge gets suppressed to the\nwest. \n\nWith PWATs well above normal, multiple thunderstorm complexes will\nprovide the opportunity for locally heavy rainfall and flash\nflooding. Confidence is too low on rainfall amounts and specific \nlocations to warrant a flood watch at this time.",
